# Fingerprint Liveness Detection (Presentation Attack Detection)

**Assignment 3: Fingerprint Liveness Detection (PAD Module)**

**Name:** Suman Raj

## Objective

The objective of this project was to develop a **Presentation Attack Detection (PAD)** module capable of classifying fingerprint images as **Live** or **Spoof** before biometric authentication. The system was implemented using transfer learning and evaluated using standard biometric performance metrics.

## Methodology

The dataset was organized into two classes: **Live** and **Spoof**, containing **40 images each (80 images total)**. The data was split into **70% training**, **15% validation**, and **15% testing**. All images were resized to **224 × 224** pixels.

To improve generalization, data augmentation techniques including random rotation, horizontal flipping, brightness adjustment, and contrast adjustment were applied during training.

The classification model was built using **MobileNetV3-Small** with ImageNet pre-trained weights. The original classification head was replaced with a binary sigmoid output layer for live/spoof classification. The model was trained using the Adam optimizer and binary cross-entropy loss.

After training, threshold calibration was performed on the validation set by sweeping thresholds from **0.00 to 1.00**. The operating threshold was selected at the point closest to the target **BPCER of 3%**. The calibrated threshold was then used to evaluate the independent test set.

## Results

| Metric                 |       Value |
| ---------------------- | ----------: |
| Operating Threshold    |    **0.99** |
| APCER                  |  **58.33%** |
| BPCER                  |   **0.00%** |
| ACER                   |  **29.17%** |
| Equal Error Rate (EER) |  **33.33%** |
| ROC-AUC                |  **68.75%** |
| Test Accuracy          |  **66.67%** |
| Precision              | **100.00%** |
| Recall                 |  **33.33%** |
| F1-Score               |  **50.00%** |

The evaluation script also generated a confusion matrix, ROC curve, score distribution plot, APCER–BPCER tradeoff curve, and evaluation metrics file.

## Discussion

The selected threshold was **0.99**, resulting in **BPCER = 0.00%** and **APCER = 58.33%** on the validation dataset. Since the validation set contained only **12 live** and **12 spoof** images, it was not possible to obtain exactly **3% BPCER**. The closest achievable operating point was therefore selected.

The model achieved a test accuracy of **66.67%** with an **EER of 33.33%**. While the model successfully classified genuine fingerprints, it showed lower performance in detecting spoof samples. This is primarily due to the limited size and diversity of the dataset.

## Conclusion

A fingerprint Presentation Attack Detection system was successfully implemented using **MobileNetV3-Small** and TensorFlow. The project demonstrates the complete PAD pipeline, including dataset preparation, transfer learning, threshold calibration, evaluation using biometric metrics (APCER, BPCER, ACER, and EER), and inference on unseen images. Future improvements include collecting a larger and more diverse dataset, incorporating additional spoof attack types, and evaluating more advanced deep learning architectures.

